<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  examples/leaky-vault/AndroidManifest.xml

  The DELIBERATELY VULNERABLE half of chapter 33's example pair. Everything in
  this file that looks wrong is wrong on purpose; examples/fixed-vault/ is the
  same app with each decision reversed, and examples/README.md walks the diff.

  Four storage-relevant decisions are made here, and only one of them is
  obviously a mistake to a reader skimming the manifest:

    android:allowBackup="true"
        This is the DEFAULT. Omitting the attribute entirely gives you the same
        behaviour, which is why "allowBackup is not set" is not a defence. It
        makes the app's credential-encrypted directory eligible for Auto Backup
        to the user's Google Drive and, on platforms that still support it, for
        extraction over USB with adb backup. On its own this is a CANDIDATE,
        not a proven leak — see README section 5.6 for what turns it into one.

    no android:dataExtractionRules / android:fullBackupContent
        With no rules file, everything in the CE directory except cache/,
        code_cache/ and no_backup/ is in scope. The fixed app ships a rules
        file that excludes the two files that matter.

    no android:directBootAware
        Correct here, and worth stating: this app has no business running
        before first unlock. The vulnerability in the fixed/leaky pair is NOT
        about Direct Boot — but the leaky app still writes to device-encrypted
        storage in Vault.java, which is the actual DE mistake, and a manifest
        audit cannot see it. See README section 3.3.

    no FLAG_SECURE anywhere
        FLAG_SECURE is a window flag, not a manifest attribute, so a manifest
        audit can never find its absence. VaultActivity sets it in the fixed
        app and not here. The consequence is a recents-thumbnail file written
        by the system, outside this app's control and outside its sandbox.
        See README section 3.9.

  AUTHORISED TESTING ONLY. Build with examples/build.sh, install on a device you
  own, and uninstall when you are done.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.pentest.ch33.vault">

    <!--
      Note what is NOT here: no READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, no WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE,
      no MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE. The app writes outside its sandbox anyway,
      because getExternalFilesDir() has needed no permission since API 19. A
      permission audit of this manifest reports a clean app. That is the single
      most useful thing this file teaches.
    -->

    <application
        android:label="CH33 Leaky Vault"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:debuggable="true"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light">

        <!--
          launchMode="singleTop" is here for a TESTING reason, not a security
          one, and it is worth knowing because it bit this chapter.

          `am start` sets FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK. With the default "standard"
          launch mode, sending a second `am start` for an activity that is
          already the top of its task does NOT deliver a new Intent: the system
          brings the existing task forward and onNewIntent is never called. adb
          prints "Activity not started, intent has been delivered to currently
          running top-most instance", which reads like success and is not.

          The consequence, if you leave this out: a lab that drives four actions
          in sequence runs only the first one, the filesystem diff shows a third
          of the expected files, and you conclude the app behaves differently
          from its source. singleTop makes onNewIntent fire every time.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".VaultActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:label="CH33 Leaky Vault">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          A second activity that exists purely so labs/lab-02 has a second,
          distinct "feature exercise" to snapshot around. Exported so a lab can
          drive it with `am start` without touching the UI.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ExportActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="CH33 Export" />
    </application>
</manifest>
